residents in china's financial hub say  they're going hungry  [Music]  [Applause]  many people have been unable to leave  their homes to buy food since late march  [Applause]  that's because shanghai officials began  a citywide lockdown to tame the  country's biggest coven 19 outbreak in  the past two years  some residents have also taken to social  media to complain  it has prompted a lot of people in  shanghai to ask  is this the right policy is the cost of  this lockdown  too great  as authorities try to tame the fast  spreading omicron variant the growing  public discontent could challenge  beijing's commitment to its dynamic zero  copied policy  [Music]  shanghai officials have acknowledged its  strategy of lockdowns mass tests and  quarantines has made it difficult for  tens of thousands of families to secure  food so they rolled out a massive effort  to deliver necessities  [Music]  local officials said there were around  11 000 couriers on the job and delivery  companies had about 100 000 other  workers  volunteers have also been going door to  door to deliver other supplies including  medicine  plus people from across the country have  been pitching in including from as far  as xinjiang where workers package bread  for residents  there's also fresh vegetables coming  from shanghai's neighboring province  but some residents have said that  they're not getting enough food from  these deliveries there are some people  who are doing okay but there's also  unquestionably people who are living on  basically the barest minimum of food  with no protein and very little in terms  of quantity for instance a family of two  received this package from the  government on the second day of the  lockdown the father and daughter said it  was all consumed in about four days and  the next delivery came about two weeks  later  so to feed themselves some people are  getting up as early as 6 am to place an  order on grocery delivery apps at a time  when supplies are more likely to be  available  this man's wife recorded him using a  massage gun to help him more quickly hit  the pay button to get his order through  before others  some residents have teamed up with  neighbors to buy in bulk we've got over  150 parcels in here  we ordered for the whole building again  or they're bartering with each other  the struggle to get food has sparked  anger in some communities  this video was posted on wei bull  china's equivalent of twitter and it was  later taken down it shows local police  trying to contain a crowd of people  weibo didn't respond to a request for  comment  these food shortages have largely  stemmed from the city's strict covid  restrictions which have confined many of  the city's food delivery workers at home  and some have chosen not to work for  fear of getting sick  also for new supplies to enter the city  truck drivers need to test negative once  you come into the city and you've  delivered your supplies it's not clear  if you can necessarily get into the  neighboring province to be able to pick  up another run of supplies because the  authorities won't  necessarily allow you into the province  because you've just come from a high  risk zone  shanghai officials have pledged to do  better  however the anxiety of not having enough  to eat is raising some collective  memories from china's famine about five  decades ago china's modern history has  had a lot of instances of deprivation  and of quite serious lack of access to  food this is a 21st century twist on  that but it has reminded people of the  importance of having stockpiles  and the situation in shanghai has  prompted some people in other cities to  prepare for the worst on weibo the topic  what to prepare if i suddenly receive a  quarantine notice has attracted more  than 200 million views  and chinese state media published this  diagram which outlines the items you  need to prepare to stay at a quarantine  center  even though shanghai officials announced  that they have lifted some measures  despite record cases people have started  to question more than just the  government's handling of the food  shortage  there is a great degree of  dissatisfaction about whether or not  it's the wisest way to deal with this  challenge this dynamic zero coveted  policy of really just trying to  eradicate the virus every time they see  it anywhere within china if it ends up  overwhelming shanghai it's going to  challenge that idea that china's  government has been more successful than  the rest of the world that containing  kovit but the experience in shanghai  hasn't prompted any indication from  beijing that it's ready to rethink its  covet strategy the head of china's coven  19 response task force said its dynamic  zero coveted policy was scientific and  would bring maximum results  so with little sign that beijing is  willing to adjust its pandemic strategy  some residents have taken to their  balconies  to shout encouragement and hope that the  lockdown ends soon  [Music]
 

